Quick mental model (3 things to always show)

Structure — you move logically: requirements → APIs → components → data flow → scaling → trade-offs.

Clarity — speak in measurable terms (RPS, latency, data size). If unknown, state assumptions with numbers.

Trade-offs & reasoning — every decision should have a “why” and an alternative you considered.

60–90 second opening script (use this verbatim)

“Thanks — to be sure I build the right system I’ll first clarify scope and assumptions, then outline core APIs, show a component diagram and the data flow for main operations, and finally cover scaling, failure modes, and trade-offs. I’ll assume X users, Y RPS, and Z data sizes unless you want to change those. Shall I proceed with those assumptions?”
(If interviewer changes numbers, adopt them. If not, proceed.)

What to say for assumptions (one-liners)

“I’ll assume 10M monthly active users, peak 5k RPS, and average object size 1KB — changing these changes scaling choices.”

Always give concrete numbers rather than vague “large scale”.

The interview checklist (follow this order)

Clarify requirements — functional + must-have vs nice-to-have. Ask 1–2 clarifying Qs if time allows.

State assumptions (numbers). Put them on the table.

List APIs (2–6 main endpoints) with input/output and QPS for each.

Draw components: clients → API gateway → services → DB / cache / queue → storage / analytics.

Walk a request: Step-by-step data flow for one main feature (write + read).

DB design: choose SQL/NoSQL, show schema or partition key and indexing.

Scaling & HA: LB, caching, read replicas, sharding, auto-scaling triggers.

Failure handling: retries, circuit breaker, DLQ, backups, replication.

Security & compliance: auth, encryption, rate limiting.

Trade-offs & alternatives: 3–4 short trade-offs and why you chose one.

Open to deep dive: “I can deep dive into X (DB/schema/consistency) — which do you want?”

Diagram tips (draw cleanly, fast)

Use boxes and arrows. Label arrows with data (e.g., 2k RPS).

Group related services inside a box (e.g., “Backend services (microservices)”).

Use dashed arrows for async. Use lightning bolt for high-throughput paths.

If drawing on a whiteboard, write assumptions at the top left.

Short templates you can reuse (plug-and-play)

API template: POST /resource — body: { ... } — response: 201 — QPS: 500 — latency: <100ms.

DB choice template: “Use X for Y because... Alternative Z fails on A (reason).”

Cache pattern: Cache aside for reads; set TTL; invalidate on writes.

Phrases to buy time (use briefly, then act)

“Great — let me outline my approach in 30 seconds.”

“I’ll state my assumptions first so my design has concrete constraints.”

Don’t overuse “umm” or silent pauses — speak while you draw.

How to handle follow-up “deep dive” requests

If asked to deep dive DB: show schema, primary key, partition key, indexes, read/write patterns, and how you’d shard.

If asked about consistency: explain CAP trade-off and pick (CP/AP) with reasons.

If asked about cost: state approximate costs drivers (compute, storage, network) and how to reduce them.

Trade-off examples (short)

SQL vs NoSQL: choose SQL for strong relational constraints and transactions; choose NoSQL (Cassandra/Dynamo) for massive writes and scale with eventual consistency.

Sync vs Async: synchronous for critical path (auth/payment); async for notifications/analytics to decouple and improve latency.

Cache TTL vs Freshness: long TTL improves latency but risks stale reads — choose short TTL + cache-aside for critical data.

Common mistakes to avoid

Not stating assumptions or leaving them vague.

Designing for infinite scale while ignoring simple CRUD correctness.

Flooding the whiteboard with too many components — keep it minimal then add details.

Ignoring failure modes (single point of failure, data loss).

If you get stuck — the “fallback” move

Restate the problem + assumptions quickly.

Draw a minimal working system (client → service → DB + cache) and explain how you’d iterate.

Offer two clear improvements and pick one to elaborate.

Practice plan (fast, high ROI)

Do 3 mock designs in 2 hours: pick common systems (URL shortener, feed system, file upload). Each: 10–15 minute sketch + 10 minute deep dive.

Time yourself: 5–7 minutes for requirements + diagram, 5–8 minutes for data flow & DB, 3–5 minutes for scaling & trade-offs, 2–3 minutes for final wrap-up.

Get feedback from a peer or record yourself.

Killer closing lines (leave interviewers impressed)

“To summarize: this design meets the stated goals (X, Y) with expected throughput of Z RPS; main cost drivers are A and B; the next improvement I’d implement is C to enhance availability/performance.”

Ask: “Would you like me to deep dive into the data model, the scaling strategy, or operational concerns?”

One-page cheat-sheet (memorize)

Clarify → Assume (numbers) → APIs → Diagram → Walk request → DB → Scale → Failures → Trade-offs → Ask for deep-dive.


