High-Level Design (HLD) Cheat-Sheet — One Page
Step 1: Opening / Script

“Thanks! I’ll clarify assumptions, outline core APIs, show a component diagram, walk through a request, and then cover scaling, failures, and trade-offs. I’ll assume X users, Y RPS, Z data size unless you suggest otherwise.”

Step 2: Clarify & Assume

Functional requirements (FR) — core features.

Non-functional requirements (NFR) — scale, latency, availability.

Assumptions with numbers:

Users: 10M monthly

RPS: 5k peak

Data size: 1KB / object

Step 3: Core APIs
API	Input	Output	QPS	Latency
POST /posts	{userId, text}	201 Created	500	<100ms
GET /feed	{userId}	List<Post>	1000	<200ms
Step 4: Component Diagram

Client → API Gateway → Microservices → DB + Cache → Queue → Storage/Analytics

Async: dashed arrows; high-throughput path: lightning bolts.

Keep minimal, add details only on request.

Step 5: Data Flow

Walk through a main request:

Client → API Gateway

Gateway → Service

Service → DB / Cache

Service → Queue (async) → Notification/Analytics

Response back to client

Step 6: Database Design

Choose SQL / NoSQL based on requirements.

Show tables/collections and key relationships.

Indexing & sharding for scale.

Cache for hot data (Redis/Memcached).

Step 7: Scaling & HA

Horizontal scaling: multiple service instances.

Load balancer (round-robin / sticky).

DB replication & sharding.

Caching patterns: cache-aside, TTL.

Async queues for decoupling.

Step 8: Failure Handling

Retry & circuit breakers.

Dead-letter queues for async failures.

Backup & disaster recovery.

Monitoring & logging (Prometheus + Grafana, ELK).

Step 9: Security

Auth: JWT / OAuth

Rate limiting & throttling

Encryption (at rest & in transit)

Step 10: Trade-offs / Talking Points

SQL vs NoSQL → consistency vs scale

Sync vs Async → simplicity vs latency

Cache TTL → freshness vs performance

Shard by user ID or content ID depending on access pattern

Step 11: Closing / Deep-Dive Offer

“This design meets X, Y, Z. Key cost drivers are A & B. Next improvement could be C. I can deep dive into DB design, scaling strategy, or operational concerns — which would you like?”

Step 12: Time Management

Requirements + Assumptions: 5–7 min

APIs + Diagram: 5–7 min

Data flow + DB: 5–8 min

Scaling & Failures: 3–5 min

Trade-offs + Wrap-up: 2–3 min

✅ Quick Mental Tricks

Always state assumptions with numbers.

Draw minimal boxes first, then add arrows/details.

Explain reasoning for every choice.

Keep phrases ready to buy time: “Let me outline the approach” / “I’ll assume X for scaling calculations.”